1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,025
The Doctor Who Shrank Singapore's Families, Then Became Its President

2
00:00:05,025 --> 00:00:21,350
In 1969, Singapore's Parliament passed the Voluntary Sterilisation Act without a single vote against it — a law that would go on to help engineer one of the steepest fertility declines any country has seen in peacetime.

3
00:00:21,350 --> 00:00:25,100
It wasn't pushed through by a career politician.

4
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:46,550
It was pushed through by a working obstetrician who, two years later, would be tapped by Lee Kuan Yew to become Singapore's second president — a job most people since have treated as ceremonial, a name and a signature, with no real memory of the population engineering he'd already done before he ever moved into the Istana.

5
00:00:46,550 --> 00:00:52,875
Sheares' rise inside medicine came first, and it was itself a quiet barrier-breaker.

6
00:00:52,875 --> 00:01:14,275
Born in 1907 to a Chinese mother and Eurasian father — a marriage the colonial press liked to hold up as a model of multiracial harmony — he graduated from the King Edward the Seventh College of Medicine in 1929 and built his career at Kandang Kerbau Hospital and Singapore General Hospital.

7
00:01:14,275 --> 00:01:30,075
In 1950 he became professor of obstetrics and gynaecology at the University of Malaya in Singapore, the first local doctor to hold the post in a system that had, until then, reserved it for colonial appointees.

8
00:01:30,075 --> 00:01:44,775
He'd go on to be remembered as the father of obstetrics and gynaecology in Singapore, credited with standardising the lower Caesarean section locally and developing a technique for constructing an artificial vagina.

9
00:01:44,775 --> 00:01:51,525
That clinical authority is what made him useful to the state well before he held any office.

10
00:01:51,525 --> 00:02:11,425
In 1959, as debate simmered over whether to legalise abortion to slow population growth, Sheares instead pushed voluntary sterilisation as the answer — and spent real effort correcting critics who assumed he meant something coercive; he insisted the operative word was voluntary.

11
00:02:11,425 --> 00:02:31,850
He became president of the Singapore Family Planning Association from 1960 to 1963, reviving an organisation that had been largely dormant since its founding in 1949, and folded family planning services directly into his own hospital's clinical practice.

12
00:02:31,850 --> 00:02:43,650
The 1969 Voluntary Sterilisation Act was the capstone: it gave the "Stop at Two" campaign an actual medical backbone, not just a slogan on a poster.

13
00:02:43,650 --> 00:02:51,350
Two years after passing that Act through Parliament, Sheares was pulled out of his own clinical career for good.

14
00:02:51,350 --> 00:03:11,000
He'd only opened a private practice two years earlier, at 63, when Lee Kuan Yew approached him about the presidency; he took the office on the 2nd of January 1971 and went on to be elected three times, more than any other president, before dying in office in May 1981.

15
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:29,225
The role itself was largely ceremonial by design — Singapore's presidency then carried little of the executive power it holds today — but the man who filled it had already done more to reshape the country's demographic future than most of the politicians serving around him.

16
00:03:29,225 --> 00:03:35,800
The government has spent the years since trying to undo exactly what Sheares' generation built.

17
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:54,100
"Have 3 or More" in 1987, cash incentives, tax rebates, matched savings for newborns, an entire Ministry-level population strategy — none of it has come close to reversing the trend, and the total fertility rate has instead kept sliding to record lows.

18
00:03:54,100 --> 00:04:04,800
The machinery Sheares helped design turned out to work far better, and for far longer, than anyone building it in 1969 seems to have expected.

19
00:04:04,800 --> 00:04:30,475
Where it fits in the bigger story: Singapore now spends heavily trying to talk people back into the exact family size Benjamin Sheares spent the 1960s convincing them to move away from — and the man responsible for most of that original engineering is remembered today, if at all, as a name on a ceremonial title rather than the architect of a demographic shift the country still hasn't recovered from.
